## =============== set =============

- A set is a built-in Python data type used to store unique elements in data types or same datatypes.

- Stores unique values (no duplicates)
- Unordered (items have no fixed position)
- It automatically removes duplicate values 
- it is useful for fast membership testing and set operations like union and intersection
- Does not support indexing or slicing
- Elements must be hashable (e.g., numbers, strings, tuples)


##  ======================= set methods ==================

| Method                   | Description                                  |
| ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |
| `add(x)`                 | Add one element                              |
| `update(iterable)`       | Add multiple elements                        |
| `remove(x)`              | Remove element (error if absent)             |
| `discard(x)`             | Remove element (no error if absent)          |
| `pop()`                  | Remove a random element                      |
| `clear()`                | Remove all elements                          |
| `copy()`                 | Return a copy of the set                     |
| `union()`                | Combine two sets                             |
| `intersection()`         | Return common elements                       |
| `difference()`           | Return elements in one set but not the other |
| `symmetric_difference()` | Return elements present in only one set      |

##  ===== frozen set ==========

- A frozenset is an immutable version of a set.

- Stores unique elements
- Unordered
- No duplicate values
- Cannot add, remove, or update elements after creation


